  "lineage": "Dominican-Republic national-couple-dance and rhythm 1850s-onward, codified into modern merengue-típico by mid-20th-century with three-instrument perico-ripiao canonical-ensemble: tambora two-headed barrel-drum, güira metallic-scraper, and accordion (originally diatonic German button-accordion imported via late-19th-century Hamburg-trade routes). The 2/4 fast-tempo merengue-clave (one-bar pattern: down-down-up-down on tambora) accelerates and accents the second eighth-note. The 1960s-1980s era added brass-section merengue-orquesta (modeled on Cuban big-band mambo) and electric-bass-and-keyboards, culminating in the international 1980s-merengue-mainstream wave. Three regional-variants: merengue-típico-cibaeño (Cibao-region accordion-led), merengue-de-orquesta (urban-Santo-Domingo big-band), and merengue-de-calle (1990s-onward street-and-urban variant).",
